{"id": "4995152a-9125-43ba-b2f1-be6c06b31075", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review how guardrails are set up on your Bedrock support assistant. That means finding the guardrails that exist, checking whether they're attached to the assistant, and checking what they actually cover. I'll report back when I have the results.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_WgS63a71ovOmx4Rj8JPqOV", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-guardrail-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Read-only review of Amazon Bedrock guardrail posture for a customer-facing support assistant. Determine whether anything is actually protecting it and whether it is exposed.",
  "context": "A user asked whether the customer-facing support assistant built on Bedrock is protected by guardrails. The assistant could be a Bedrock Agent, a Bedrock Flow, a Lambda/ECS app calling InvokeModel/Converse, or a Knowledge Base RetrieveAndGenerate setup. Identify which it is. Shallow, focused pass; do not make any mutating calls.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 123456789012 (agent space account; also check other associated accounts via list_associations)", "Region us-east-1 first; also check other regions where Bedrock agents/guardrails exist if cheap to do"],
  "criteria": "1) Inventory guardrails (bedrock list_guardrails / get_guardrail, including versions): which filters are configured and at what strength (content filters incl. PROMPT_ATTACK, denied topics, word filters, sensitive information/PII filters, contextual grounding checks, automated reasoning if present), and whether any are only DRAFT version / never versioned. 2) Inventory Bedrock agents (bedrock-agent list_agents/get_agent) and identify the customer support assistant; check guardrailConfiguration on each agent, and on flows/knowledge bases where applicable. 3) Check for application-level enforcement signals: Lambda functions / ECS services that call bedrock-runtime and whether they pass guardrailIdentifier (look at env vars/config only, do not read secrets); IAM policies that condition bedrock:InvokeModel on bedrock:GuardrailIdentifier if visible via read-only calls. 4) Check model invocation logging config (get_model_invocation_logging_configuration) and whether guardrail intervention metrics exist in CloudWatch namespace AWS/Bedrock/Guardrails (recent 7 days, InvocationsIntervened etc.). 5) Conclude with: what protects the assistant today, gaps/exposures (e.g., no guardrail attached, prompt-attack filter missing or LOW, no PII filters, no grounding checks, draft-only version, output not covered), and prioritized recommendations. Clearly flag what you could not verify."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["discovering-topology", "exploring-metrics"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review Bedrock guardrail coverage for support assistant"}], "created_at": null}

